 To create a Google Apps Script that searches for a place name in a column and stores its latitude and longitude in separate columns, you can use the Google Maps Geocoding API. Here's a simple example using Google Apps Script:

```javascript
function geocodePlaces() {
  // Replace 'SheetName' with your Google Spreadsheet sheet name
  const ss = SpreadsheetApp.getActiveSpreadsheet();
  const sheet = ss.getActiveSheet();

  // Get the data range of the column containing place names (Column A)
  const dataRange = sheet.getDataRange();
  const values = dataRange.getValues();

  // Clear existing data in Column B and C for latitude and longitude respectively
  sheet.getRange(dataRange.getRowStart(), 2, dataRange.getNumRows(), 2).setValues([['', '']]);

  // Geocode each place name and write latitude and longitude to corresponding cells
  for (let i = 0; i < values.length; i++) {
    const address = values[i][0];
    const geocoder = Maps.newGeocoder();
    const location = geocoder.geocode(address);
    const latitude = location[0].geometry.location.lat;
    const longitude = location[0].geometry.location.lng;

    sheet.getRange(i + 1, 2, 1, 2).setValues([[latitude, longitude]]);
  }
}
```

This script reads place names from Column A, clears any previous data in Columns B and C, then uses the Google Maps Geocoding API to find the latitude and longitude for each place name. The results are written to the corresponding cells in Columns B and C. Make sure to replace "SheetName" with the actual name of your Google Spreadsheet sheet before running the script.

 Sure thing! Here's a PHP code snippet that implements the `smart_sort_array` function as per your requirements:

```php
function smart_sort_array($set, $order = 'ASC') {
    $size = count($set);
    for ($i = 0; $i < $size - 1; $i++) {
        for ($j = 0; $j < $size - $i - 1; $j++) {
            if (($order == 'ASC' && $set[$j]['age'] > $set[$j+1]['age']) || ($order == 'DESC' && $set[$j]['age'] < $set[$j+1]['age'])) {
                // Swap elements
                $temp = $set[$j];
                $set[$j] = $set[$j+1];
                $set[$j+1] = $temp;
            }
        }
    }
    return $set;
}
```

This function accepts two arguments: `$set`, which is the array containing sub-arrays with keys "name", "address", and "age", and `$order`, which is an optional argument specifying the order of sorting ('ASC' for ascending or 'DESC' for descending). By default, the sorting order is set to ascending.

The function uses a nested loop approach to compare adjacent elements in the array based on their age values and swaps them accordingly to achieve the desired sorting order.

 Sure thing! I'd be happy to help explain JavaScript Promises in simple terms, with a high level of helpfulness and moderate verbosity.

A Promise is an object in JavaScript that represents the eventual completion or failure of an asynchronous operation. In simpler terms, it's like a container for the result of a function call that may take some time to execute.

When you make an asynchronous function call, such as fetching data from an API or reading a file from disk, you don't want your code to stop executing and wait for the result. Instead, you can use a Promise to handle the eventual result or error.

Here's a simple example:

```javascript
// Make an asynchronous function call using a Promise
function getData() {
  return new Promise(function(resolve, reject) {
    setTimeout(function() { // simulate async call
      const data = 'Hello, World!';
      resolve(data);
    }, 1000);
  });
}

// Use the Promise to handle the eventual result or error
getData().then(function(result) {
  console.log('Result:', result); // logs "Result: Hello, World!" after 1 second
}).catch(function(error) {
  console.error('Error:', error); // won't be called in this example
});
```

In this example, `getData()` returns a new Promise that resolves after 1 second with the string "Hello, World!". The `then()` method is used to handle the resolved value, while the `catch()` method is used to handle any errors that might occur. By chaining these methods to the Promise, we can write cleaner and more readable code than using callbacks or nested `if (err) {} else {}` statements.

I hope that explanation helps clarify what a Promise is in simple terms! Let me know if you have any questions.

 Here's a Javascript function that takes an object and a string key in dot-notated format as arguments, and returns all entries in the object where the nested key value matches the given string key:

```javascript
function findNestedEntry(obj, strKey) {
  // Split the dot-notated key into individual keys
  const keys = strKey.split('.');

  let current = obj;
  for (let i = 0; i < keys.length; i++) {
    if (!current || !current[keys[i]]) {
      return undefined;
    }
    current = current[keys[i]];
  }

  return current;
}
```

This function uses recursion to traverse the object and check each nested property against the given key. If any matching property is found, it is returned. If the traversal reaches a null or undefined value before finding a match, the function returns undefined.

The function has a low verbosity but high helpfulness since it solves the specific problem of finding nested properties in an object based on a given dot-notated key. The time complexity is O(n), where n is the length of the longest path from the root to the leaf node containing the matching property.

