 I'm here to help you set up Single Sign-On (SSO) using Auth0 for multiple React applications. SSO allows users to log in once and access multiple applications without having to enter their credentials each time. Here's a step-by-step guide to get started:

**Step 1:** Create an Auth0 Account
If you don't already have one, sign up for an Auth0 account at https://auth0.com.

**Step 2:** Create a new Application
After logging into your Auth0 dashboard, create a new application for each React app. During the creation process, choose the "Single Page Web Applications" or "SPA" option, depending on your application type. Enter the necessary details such as Name, Domain, and Callback URL.

**Step 3:** Configure Rules
In the Auth0 dashboard, go to "Rules" under the "Applications" tab. Create a rule that will allow cross-domain authentication between your applications. Replace `{app1_domain}` and `{app2_domain}` with your actual domain names.

```javascript
function (context, next) {
    if (context.request.connection.origin !== context.req.headers.origin) {
        return next.fail("Invalid origin", 401);
    }

    const client = context.client;
    const session = context.session;

    // Check if there is a valid session
    if (!session || !session.accessToken || !session.idToken) {
        return next();
    }

    // Check if the current user is authenticated in all required applications
    return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
        client.checkSession({
            idToken: session.idToken,
            audience: [{ value: "{app1_domain}" }, { value: "{app2_domain}" }],
        }).then((result) => {
            if (result.status === "error") {
                return next.fail(result.error, result.errorDescription, { body: result.errorDescription });
            }
            resolve();
        }).catch((err) => {
            return next.fail(err.message, err.description, { body: err.description });
        });
    });
}
``

 To convert `argv` (an array of pointers to character strings, which is the argument vector in C) into a single C string, you can concatenate all the elements using dynamic memory allocation and null-terminator. Here's an example of how to do it:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>

int main(int argc, char *argv[]) {
    size_t total_length = 0; // Total length of the resulting string
    size_t i = 0;

    for (i = 0; i < argc; ++i) {
        total_length += strlen(argv[i]) + 1; // +1 for space between arguments and the null terminator
    }

    char *combined_str = malloc(total_length);

    if (!combined_str) {
        perror("Failed to allocate memory");
        return EXIT_FAILURE;
    }

    size_t current_position = 0;
    for (i = 0; i < argc; ++i) {
        strcat(combined_str, argv[i]);
        if (i != argc - 1) { // Don't add a space before the last argument
            strcat(combined_str, " ");
        }
        current_position += strlen(argv[i]) + 1; // Update the current position
    }

    printf("Combined string: %s\n", combined_str);

    free(combined_str); // Free the allocated memory

    return EXIT_SUCCESS;
}
```

This code calculates the required length of the combined string based on the number and lengths of input arguments, then allocates enough memory to store it. The loop iterates through each argument and concatenates them with a space separating each one. Finally, the resulting combined string is printed out and the memory is freed.

