 I. Introduction

In this PowerShell script, we will recursively check the Access Control Lists (ACLs) of all folders in a specified path to determine if any Access Control Entries (ACEs) reference Security Identifiers (SIDs) that occur only in the SIDHistory of any user or group in the Active Directory domain. The script utilizes the `System.Security.Principal.SecurityIdentifier` class and the `Get-ADObject` cmdlet to query Active Directory for users and groups with SIDHistory information.

II. Script Preparation

Before running the script, make sure your PowerShell environment is properly configured with the Active Directory module for PowerShell (RSAT). If you haven't installed it yet, follow these instructions: https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/powershell/windows-server/identity/ad-ds/install-adfs-ps-winrm?view=ws2016-ps

Now let's create the script file named `Check-DomainSidHistory.ps1`.

```powershell
# Check-DomainSidHistory.ps1

param (
    [Parameter(Mandatory=$true)]
    [string]$Path
)

function Get-ADUserOrGroupWithSidHistory ($sid) {
    $filter = "SID -eq '$($sid)' -or SIDHistory -contains '$($sid)'"
    $usersOrGroups = Get-ADObject @("User", "Group") -Filter $filter -Properties SID, Name -SizeLimit 0 | Where-Object {$_.Name}
    return $usersOrGroups
}

function Test-FolderAcl ($folderPath) {
    $acl = Get-Acl -Path $folderPath -Recurse | Select-ExpandProperty Access | Where-Object {$_.IdentityReference.Value -match "^S-(?<sid>[^-]*):"}

    foreach ($ace in $acl) {
        $sid = $Matches.sid
        $userOrGroup = Get-ADUserOrGroupWithSidHistory $sid

        if ($null -eq $userOrGroup) {
            Write-Output "SID: $sid, Folder Path: $folderPath"
        }
    }
